'   fulcio OIDC identity     : https://docs.sigstore.dev/certificate_authority/oidc-in-fulcio/
'   sigstore security model  : https://docs.sigstore.dev/about/security/
'   spiffe vs fulcio EKU     : https://github.com/spiffe/spiffe/issues/379
'   ssh-keygen(1) verify-required : https://man.openbsd.org/ssh-keygen
'   yubikey ssh + FIDO2 for git   : https://developers.yubico.com/SSH/Securing_git_with_SSH_and_FIDO2.html
'   ykman openpgp touch policies  : https://docs.yubico.com/software/yubikey/tools/ykman/OpenPGP_Commands.html
'   keytocard resets the policy   : https://github.com/Yubico/yubikey-manager/issues/693
'   github commit signature verify: https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/managing-commit-signature-verification/about-commit-signature-verification
'   signing commits with ssh certs: https://sayr.us/git/ssh-sign-ca/
'   comparing signing options     : https://www.kenmuse.com/blog/comparing-github-commit-signing-options/
'   biscuit: offline attenuation  : https://www.clever.cloud/blog/engineering/2021/04/12/introduction-to-biscuit/
'   macaroons (libmacaroons)      : https://github.com/rescrv/libmacaroons
'   IETF attenuating agent tokens : https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-niyikiza-oauth-attenuating-agent-tokens/
'
' ── THE QUESTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Multiple operators act on one host: humans, agents, and subagents. Give each
' a unique identifier, track it across actions that matter — adding a secret,
' making a commit — and the first use is `sbx`: prove a HUMAN operator set the
' keys, and say WHICH human.
'
' ── SCOPED 2026-09-09: ONE EXPERIMENT, THE REST DEFERRED ───────────────────
' The exploration below went wider than what is being tried. Read this first,
' because most of the file is now BACKGROUND for a decision already taken.
'
'   IN SCOPE, AND THE WHOLE EXPERIMENT
'     ssh-sk for a HUMAN operator, verified by proveo, attesting what set an
'     `sbx secret`. One key, one trust store, one namespace, no CA.
'
'   AGENTS OWN EXACTLY TWO THINGS, and provenance for both is DEFERRED
'     the TUI transcript, and their git commits.
'
'   DEFERRED, NOT REJECTED
'     how to detect that either of those is untampered; how a main agent
'     cascades identity into forked subagents; per-run certificates; the agent
'     CA; the four-class ledger.
'
' WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT CUT. The human half is the only part where the
' cryptography does what it looks like it does — a touch a headless process
' cannot produce, checkable by a verifier that can require it. Everything on the
' agent side runs into the same wall from a different angle: a signature by the
' subject over evidence about the subject proves origin and not truth. Deferring
' those is not timidity; it is declining to build machinery whose central claim
' would be false.
'
' ── THE ONE EXPERIMENT, CONCRETELY ─────────────────────────────────────────
' Everything needed is on the operator's desk — three YubiKeys — and nothing
' here needs a CA, an IdP or a new format.
'
'   1  a resident key with verification required
'        ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk -O resident -O verify-required \
'          -C "proveo executor" -f ~/.ssh/id_proveo_executor
'   2  an allowed_signers line that REQUIRES what the key offers
'        <email> namespaces="proveo-executor" verify-required sk-ssh-ed25519@... AAAA...
'   3  attest one secret, at the moment proveo stores it
'        printf '%s' "$digest_of_the_value" | ssh-keygen -Y sign \
'          -f ~/.ssh/id_proveo_executor -n proveo-executor
'   4  proveo verifies it, and REFUSES an unverified signature
'        ssh-keygen -Y verify -f allowed_signers -I <email> -n proveo-executor -s sig
'
' WHAT EACH STEP IS ACTUALLY TESTING, because a green result proves little on
' its own:
'   step 1  that a resident sk key can be made at all on this hardware
'   step 3  THAT A TOUCH AND A PIN ARE DEMANDED. If either is silent, F1 has
'           failed and the experiment is over.
'   step 4  THAT `verify-required` IS ENFORCED BY THE CHECKER. Repeat it with a
'           plain `ssh-ed25519` key under the SAME principal and confirm the
'           verify FAILS. A pass there is the whole point; a pass in step 3
'           alone measures the hardware, not the guarantee.
'
' The negative case in step 4 is the one that must not be skipped. It is the
' same shape as every false green this tree has catalogued: without it, the
' experiment measures that signing works, which nobody doubted.
'
' ── THREE YUBIKEYS: ENROLMENT IS PLURAL, AND THAT IS NOT A CONVENIENCE ─────
' The operator has three. Recording all of them is not redundancy for comfort —
' it is the only recovery path there is.
'
' AN sk PRIVATE KEY CANNOT BE BACKED UP. It is hardware-bound by design; there
' is no export, no copy, no escrow. One enrolled key means one hardware failure
' away from being unable to sign anything ever again — and, if a retired line is
' handled badly, from being unable to VERIFY history either. So a second and
' third key are the backup, because nothing else can be.
'
' allowed_signers takes ONE LINE PER KEY, and the principals field is what ties
' them together — three lines, same email, same `namespaces` and
' `verify-required`. Any of the three signs; all three verify.
'
' `-O resident` matters for a different loss. A resident key can be pulled back
' out of the token onto a NEW HOST (`ssh-keygen -K`), so a resident enrolment
' survives losing the laptop. It does not survive losing the token, which is
' what the other two are for.
'
' ── THE RETIREMENT TRAP, AND THE OPTION THAT AVOIDS IT ─────────────────────
' Entries in the ledger were signed by a SPECIFIC key. If a token is lost and
' its allowed_signers line is DELETED, every historical entry that key signed
' stops verifying — the audit trail silently loses its oldest half, and it looks
' like tampering rather than like key rotation.
'
' `valid-before` is the answer, and it is already in the option list this file
' quotes: retire the line by bounding it in time instead of removing it. History
' verifies against a key that was valid when it signed; new signatures from that
' key do not. Rotation stops being destructive.
'
' Which also means the ledger must record WHEN an entry was signed in a form the
' verifier uses, not merely as a field in the payload — otherwise `valid-before`
' has nothing to compare against and rotation is back to deletion.
'
' ── AND THREE ENROLMENTS ARE THREE CHANCES TO PROVISION ONE WEAKLY ─────────
' Every `ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk` is an opportunity to forget
' `-O verify-required`, and with three tokens the odds triple. This is exactly
' where the verifier-side option earns its place: because `verify-required` sits
' in allowed_signers, a weakly-provisioned key FAILS VERIFICATION whatever was
' chosen at keygen. The guarantee does not depend on remembering a flag.
'
' STILL OPEN: whether the three share ONE principal or get distinguishable ones.
' One principal is simpler and treats the keys as interchangeable; separate
' principals record WHICH token signed, which is what a targeted revocation
' needs after a single token is lost. The F1 probe should measure both, since
' the allowed_signers grammar supports either and the cost is a line.
'
' ── WHAT AGENTS OWN, AND WHY BOTH ARE DEFERRED ─────────────────────────────
'   THE TUI TRANSCRIPT   agent-writable today, measured. The two candidate
'                        answers are already known — narrow the home mount so
'                        `logs/` is not exposed (PREVENTS), or have proveo digest
'                        the stream as it writes it (DETECTS). Neither needs a
'                        key, which is exactly why this is deferred out of an
'                        identity experiment: it is a mount and a hash, not a
'                        signature.
'   GIT COMMITS          the agent authors them, so a signature attributes and
'                        cannot validate. The forge is irrelevant here, so a
'                        local verifier could check a plain agent key — but
'                        deciding WHAT such a signature would mean comes before
'                        producing one.
'
' ── DEFERRED: THE CASCADE INTO FORKED SUBAGENTS ────────────────────────────
' Not designed here, but the SHAPE of the answer is constrained enough to write
' down, so the deferral is informed rather than blank.
'
' THE ONE INVARIANT: a parent cannot mint authority it does not hold. Any
' cascade must ATTENUATE — the child's authority a subset of the parent's — and
' a scheme that lets a fork widen its own scope is worse than no scheme, because
' it launders authority while looking rigorous.
'
' THREE CANDIDATES, ranked by how well they fit a FORK rather than a service:
'   biscuit tokens     public-key signatures plus OFFLINE ATTENUATION: a holder
'                      adds caveats and derives a strictly weaker token with no
'                      issuer round trip. A fork is exactly that operation, and
'                      Datalog checks carry the narrowing inside the token.
'   macaroons          the original of the idea: HMAC chaining, attenuation by
'                      caveat, and explicitly NO proof of possession — so a
'                      leaked token is a usable token. Simpler and weaker.
'   ssh certs + a CA   what the earlier sections designed. Delegation works, but
'                      every fork needs an ISSUER round trip to the host, which
'                      is the wrong shape for something that forks freely.
'
' AND THE STRONGEST ARGUMENT FOR WAITING: the IETF has an active draft on this
' exact problem — "Attenuating Authorization Tokens for Agentic Delegation
' Chains", draft-niyikiza-oauth-attenuating-agent-tokens-01:
'     https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-niyikiza-oauth-attenuating-agent-tokens/
' Inventing a cascade now means maintaining a private one against a standard
' that is arriving. NOT READ IN FULL — found by search and cited for its
' existence and title, which is what the deferral rests on; whether its model
' fits a fork is exactly the question to ask when this stops being deferred.
'
' ── THE DISTINCTION THAT DECIDES EVERYTHING: CUSTODY IS NOT PRESENCE ────────
' A GPG key in `~/.gnupg`, or a live `ssh-agent` socket, is usable by EVERY
' process running as that user. On proveo's host that includes proveo itself and
' anything it launches. So a signature from a software key proves CUSTODY of the
' key and never that a human was there — and an agent holding the same key signs
' identically. "Give each operator a GPG key" does not, on its own, answer the
' question that motivates it.
'
' OpenSSH's own guidance says so about the escape hatch: generating an sk key
' with `no-touch-required` "discards the guarantee that a human deliberately
' authorized each signature and is not recommended for supply-chain use."
'
' So the mechanisms that CAN answer "was a human there" are the ones a headless
' process cannot satisfy:
'   USER PRESENCE       FIDO2 / `sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com` with touch
'                       required. A physical interaction happened.
'   USER VERIFICATION   presence plus PIN or biometric. Yubico: the
'                       authenticator "verifies that the user is authorized to
'                       use the authenticator, and signals to the RP whether
'                       user verification was successful."
'   SECURE ENCLAVE      macOS Touch ID over a keychain item, whose read
'                       "inherits Apple's trust" (secret-references.puml)
'
' ── AMENDED: GPG ON A YUBIKEY *CAN* ASSERT PRESENCE ────────────────────────
' The section above over-generalised, and an operator with three YubiKeys caught
' it. The YubiKey OpenPGP applet has its OWN touch policy — `ykman openpgp keys
' set-touch sig on` — so a card-resident GPG key CAN require a physical touch.
' "GPG proves custody" is true of a SOFTWARE key and false of that setup.
'
' But the objection to GPG here is not only humanity, and the rest survives:
'
'   OFF BY DEFAULT        the touch policy for OpenPGP slots ships `off`. Nothing
'                         about using a YubiKey implies presence was required.
'   A 15-SECOND WINDOW    `cached` and `cached-fixed` require a touch and then
'                         CACHE it for 15 seconds. One touch, then a burst of
'                         silent signatures — which is exactly the shape an
'                         agent would exploit.
'   THE AGENT CACHES TOO  gpg-agent caches interactions by default; fresh
'                         interaction needs `default-cache-ttl 0` and
'                         `max-cache-ttl 0` in gpg-agent.conf.
'   NOT IN THE SIGNATURE  the policy is a property of the key, not of the
'                         signature. A verifier cannot tell whether a touch was
'                         required, so the guarantee is unenforceable
'                         downstream. This is F1, and for OpenPGP it is OPEN.
'   IT SILENTLY REGRESSES `gpg keytocard` has been measured resetting the
'                         OpenPGP touch policy to OFF
'                         (Yubico/yubikey-manager#693), and `ykman openpgp
'                         reset` leaves the attestation key's cached-fixed
'                         policy in place (#642). Ordinary key management
'                         changes the security property without saying so.
'
' ── AND SSH-SK ANSWERS F1, WHICH IS THE DECIDING DIFFERENCE ────────────────
' OpenSSH's `verify-required` "indicates that a private key should require user
' verification for each signature ... Signatures made using this key indicate
' that the user was first verified, e.g. by PIN or on-token biometrics" (8.4+).
'
' The decisive part is WHERE that option lives. The allowed_signers file is
' "patterned after the authorized_keys file format", with fields principals,
' OPTIONS, keytype, key — so `verify-required` is a VERIFIER-SIDE requirement,
' not a keygen-time preference. `ssh-keygen -Y verify -f allowed_signers` will
' refuse a signature that does not assert user verification.
'
' That inverts the F1 risk. For ssh-sk the guarantee is enforceable by the party
' who cares; for OpenPGP it is chosen by the party being checked. Everything
' else about the two formats is secondary to that asymmetry.
'
' ── ALTERNATIVES, AND WHAT EACH ONE ACTUALLY VERIFIES ──────────────────────
'   ssh-sk + allowed_signers   presence by construction, verification on demand,
'   `verify-required`          and ENFORCED AT VERIFY TIME. Native to a YubiKey,
'   (RECOMMENDED)              `gpg.format = ssh` in git, no daemon to misconfigure.
'                              Identity is a principal in a file the repo keeps.
'   PIV / x509                 `gpg.format = x509` with smimesign. The PIV applet
'                              has its own touch policy, and an org-issued cert
'                              chain carries the name. Heavier; needs a CA.
'   Sigstore gitsign           keyless: OIDC identity in a short-lived cert, plus
'                              a transparency log. The IdP asserts WHICH human
'                              rather than a local mapping — the strongest answer
'                              to that half — at the cost of needing an IdP and
'                              publishing identities to a public log.
'   Secure Enclave SSH         Touch ID presence with no extra hardware, and this
'                              tree already leans on Apple's trust for keychain
'                              reads. No verifier-side enforcement of presence.
'
' A key per operator is therefore the wrong unit. The unit is A SIGNATURE WHOSE
' PRESENCE CLASS THE VERIFIER CAN REQUIRE, and only two of the four above offer
' that.
'
' ── "WHICH HUMAN" IS A SECOND QUESTION, WITH A DIFFERENT MECHANISM ──────────
' Presence says somebody touched a key. It does not say who. Two ways to bind a
' name, and they have different trust roots:
'   the key's own principal   an email or principal attached to the signing key.
'                             Only as good as the mapping the repo keeps, and
'                             the operator asserts it themselves.
'   an OIDC identity          Fulcio issues a short-lived certificate binding an
'                             ephemeral key to an OpenID Connect identity, whose
'                             subject alternative name "could be an email,
'                             SPIFFE ID, or GitHub Actions workflow identity".
'                             The IdP asserts the name, not the operator.
'
' ── CAN AN agent-cert PROTECT THE AGENT'S OWN EVIDENCE? NO ─────────────────
' The question was whether a per-run certificate could make a run's evidence —
' TUI transcript, network log, observability, resource monitoring — tamper-proof,
' "so only the owner of the key can write it". It cannot, and the reason is
' structural rather than a gap to close.
'
' A SIGNATURE AUTHENTICATES ORIGIN, NOT TRUTH. An agent holding a key can write
' false evidence and sign it perfectly, omit an event and sign the omission, or
' re-sign an edited transcript. Nothing about the signature distinguishes an
' honest log from a doctored one, because in both cases the author IS the
' signer. YOU CANNOT PROTECT EVIDENCE ABOUT A SUBJECT WITH A KEY HELD BY THAT
' SUBJECT.
'
' And "only the key owner can write it" is a FILESYSTEM property, not a
' cryptographic one. A key does not gate writes; it gates BELIEVABLE writes. The
' write itself is gated by the mount.
'
' ── MEASURED PROBLEM 2: A SUBAGENT IS NOT A PRINCIPAL YET ──────────────────
' `defs/subagents/*.md` are PROMPT FILES, composed into the agent by
' `render_subagents` during the seed. There is no process boundary, no separate
' credential, and nothing for a certificate to be issued TO. So the third level
' of the model has nothing to stand on today, and the earlier framing —
' "certified by the same CA under `subagent:*`" — describes a world that does
' not exist here.
'
' What IS available is the agent asserting it: "I, agent:<sid>, invoked
' architect at T". That is self-asserted and must be LABELLED as such, which
' extends the same idea one rung further — the ledger carries a CLASS, and the
' class says how much the trust store actually backs:
'
'     human-verified   sk key, `verify-required` satisfied
'     human-present    sk key, presence only
'     agent-cert       a per-run certificate the agent could not mint itself
'     agent-asserted   a claim the agent makes about itself; nothing backs it
'                      beyond the signature on the containing entry
'
' Four classes, one of which is deliberately weak and says so. That is more
' useful than three classes where the third is fictional.
'
' ── WHAT THIS REPO ALREADY CLAIMS AND CANNOT PROVE ──────────────────────────
' Every commit in this tree carries `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5
' <noreply@anthropic.com>`. That is an assertion of agent authorship, written by
' the agent, in a trailer anyone can type. And `gitidentity.Resolve` produces a
' name and email by precedence over the environment and git config: a CLAIM,
' resolved, never attested.
'
' So the move this experiment proposes is narrow and specific — from CLAIMED to
' ATTESTED — and the repo is already making the claims. Nothing new is being
' asserted; what is missing is anything behind it.
'
' ── THE SANDBOX BOUNDARY IS THE ASSET, AND ALSO THE INVARIANT ──────────────
' The agent runs in a VM with no path to the host's authenticator. THAT is what
' would make "a human set this" forgery-resistant rather than merely
' conventional: the agent cannot touch a key it cannot reach.
'
' Which turns the whole scheme into one checkable invariant — never forward the
' authenticator inward:
'   no `ssh-agent` forwarding into a sandbox
'   no USB / HID passthrough to the guest
'   no mounting `~/.gnupg`, and no gpg-agent socket
' Each is a hole that silently converts an attestation back into a claim. This
' is the part that must be TESTED rather than documented, because it is the
' assumption every other line here rests on.
'
' ── EXPERIMENTS — F1 AND F2 ARE LIVE; THE REST ARE DEFERRED ────────────────
' F1  CAN THIS HOST PRODUCE A PRESENCE ASSERTION A VERIFIER CAN REQUIRE?
'     ANSWERED FROM THE DOCS, NOT YET PROBED, AND THE ANSWER SPLITS BY FORMAT:
'       ssh-sk    YES. `verify-required` is an allowed_signers OPTION, so
'                 `ssh-keygen -Y verify` refuses a signature that does not
'                 assert user verification. Enforced by the checker.
'       OpenPGP   NO. The touch policy is a property of the key and does not
'                 travel in the signature, so a verifier cannot require it.
'     WHAT IS STILL UNMEASURED is the local behaviour: that a touch is actually
'     demanded on this operator's three YubiKeys, that `-Y verify` really
'     refuses a non-UV signature, and what a `cached` OpenPGP policy looks like
'     from outside during its 15-second window.
'     AND THE PLURAL CASE: three lines under one principal all verifying, a
'     `valid-before` line still verifying its own history while refusing new
'     signatures, and whether distinguishable principals per token are worth the
'     extra lines. See the enrolment section above.
'
' F2  IS THE AUTHENTICATOR UNREACHABLE FROM INSIDE A SANDBOX?
'     Attempt the same signature from inside a run and expect it to FAIL. A pass
'     here is a defect, not a success. This is the invariant above, and it is
'     the cheapest experiment of the five.
'
' F3  WHAT BINDS A NAME ON A HOST THAT HAS NO IdP?
'     Measure what identity material actually exists — git config name and
'     email, an sk key's principals, any OIDC path — against what a signature
'     can bind. A solo operator with no IdP is the common case, and a scheme
'     that only works with Fulcio is a scheme for somebody else.
'
' F4  DEFERRED — WHAT IS A SUBAGENT, BEFORE ASKING WHAT SIGNS FOR ONE?
'     REFRAMED AGAIN, and downward. `defs/subagents/*.md` are prompt files
'     composed into one process, so there is no principal to certify and the
'     issuing-path question is premature. The question ahead of it is whether a
'     subagent should BECOME a process — which is a capability decision with
'     costs far beyond provenance — or whether `agent-asserted` is the honest
'     ceiling for that level. Answer that before designing an issuer.
'
' F4b DEFERRED — CAN THE HOST MINT A PER-RUN CERTIFICATE ON REQUEST?
'     REFRAMED. The signature format handles delegation — `cert-authority` plus
'     dual principal validation — so the open question is no longer "can a
'     subagent be distinguished" but "what does the issuing path look like".
'     An in-sandbox process has to ask the host to mint a cert for it, and that
'     request is the new surface: what it may ask for, what bounds the principal
'     it receives, and whether a subagent can request a cert broader than its
'     parent's. Measure the issuer, not the format.
'
' F6  DEFERRED WITH THE CERTS — DOES `-Y verify` ACCEPT A CERT-SIGNED SIG?
'     The whole agent and subagent design rests on it. The man page describes
'     verifying "signatures made by certificates" and third parties report certs
'     "working in the CLI", so this is DOCUMENTED-NOT-PROBED — and it is the
'     cheapest of the six to settle: mint a CA, issue an `agent:test` cert, sign
'     in a `proveo-agent` namespace, and verify against a `cert-authority`
'     line. If it fails, the three-class design collapses to plain keys and the
'     subagent level loses its delegation again.
'
' F5  PARTLY ANSWERED, REST DEFERRED — WHERE DOES THE LEDGER REST?
'     PARTLY ANSWERED FROM THE CODE. `run.StateDir()` is host-only — not in the
'     mount list — so the ledger goes there and an agent cannot reach it. The
'     TRANSCRIPT is the opposite: it lives in the home mount, read-write, so it
'     is the artifact most in need of a rolling digest rather than a
'     teardown one.
'     STILL TO DECIDE: signatures make forgery detectable and not deletion
'     impossible, so a missing entry must FAIL CLOSED — treated as unattested —
'     or removing the ledger becomes the attack rather than forging it.
'
' ── FIRST APPLICABLE USE: sbx SECRET PROVENANCE ─────────────────────────────
' This answers the one blocker left open in host-held-provider-keys.puml. When
' proveo runs `sbx secret set <service>`, it records an attestation BESIDE the
' store — never inside it, and never the value:
'
'     service      the kebab-case service id sbx resolves
'     fingerprint  a digest of the value written
'     when         and by what tool
'     identity     the attested operator, and the PRESENCE CLASS: human with
'                  verification, human with presence only, agent, subagent, or
'                  none
'
' The seal is untouched, which is the point: the ledger holds a claim ABOUT a
' value, never the value, so Blocker 1's security property survives intact.
'
' AND IT ANSWERS THE SENTENCE THE INCIDENT WAS MISSING. `sbx secret ls` said
' `xai` existed. Both names it printed were correct. A ledger would have said
' "set by a credprobe test fixture, 2026-09-07, presence class: none" — which is
' exactly what nobody could see before that entry authenticated a run and its
' provider answered 403.
'
' ── WHAT IT CANNOT DO, NAMED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER ───────────────────
' IT CANNOT VERIFY THE STORE'S CONTENT. There is no `get`, so proveo can only
' compare the ledger's fingerprint against a candidate key it resolves from the
' environment. "Which key is stored" therefore stays answerable only as "does
' your env still resolve to what was attested" — narrower than it sounds, and
' still enough for the incident.
'
' A FINGERPRINT IS A VERIFIER FOR A GUESSABLE SECRET. Provider API keys are
' high-entropy, so a plain digest is safe; an HMAC under a per-host salt also
' defeats cross-host correlation, at the cost of no longer comparing between
' machines. Pick one deliberately rather than by default.
'
' PRESENCE IS NOT INTENT. A touch proves a human was at the authenticator when
' something was signed. It does not prove they read what they signed, and no
' mechanism here can. Any claim stronger than "a human was present" is a claim
' this scheme does not support.
' ── WHAT WAS CUT FROM THIS FILE, AND WHERE IT WENT ─────────────────────────
' This file reached 840 lines by amendment — a GPG correction, ssh-sk, a
' three-level design, a forge constraint, an audit trail, a scope cut, plural
' enrolment — and after the scope cut most of that was background under a
' decision. The background is deleted rather than archived, with its findings
' named here so nothing is silently lost:
'
'   the certificate design, the three key classes, the agent CA, `namespaces` as
'     a separation of powers, and the agent-signs-a-commit case — all DEFERRED
'     by the scope cut, so the design that served them is gone. What survives is
'     the reason they defer: a signature by the subject over evidence about the
'     subject authenticates origin and not truth.
'   the transcript being agent-writable, who holds the pen for each kind of
'     evidence, the measured `/proveo-home/logs/` reading and the mount-not-the-
'     key remedy — MOVED to _spec/_paradigms/workspace-boundary.puml, because
'     they are instances of a class four plans share and not properties of an
'     ssh-sk experiment.
'
' ── AND THE TWO LEDGERS STAY SEPARATE, DECIDED ─────────────────────────────
' Whether this file's attestation ledger and agent-council-ledger.puml's work
' ledger should be ONE artifact was left open in both. Decided: SEPARATE.
'
' They want the same mechanism — append-only, hash-chained, per-writer identity
' — and merging them would be tidy. It would also make a council's coordination
' store the same artifact as the provenance record for credentials, and those
' have very different blast radii: a coordination ledger is written constantly
' by every run and read for scheduling, while a provenance ledger is written
' rarely, by a human with a touch, and read when trust is in question. One
' artifact means one compromise reaches both, and one retention policy has to
' serve both.
'
' Recorded here so it stops resurfacing as an open question in either file.
'
title EXPERIMENT — ssh-sk for the human operator;\nthe agent and cascade halves deferred

actor "human operator" as HUMAN
node "the host" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "authenticator\nFIDO2 touch, then PIN" as AUTH <<app>>
  component "**allowed_signers**\nthe trust boundary —\n""verify-required"" lives HERE" as ALLOW <<app>>
  component "software key\n""~/.gnupg"" · ssh-agent socket" as SOFT <<error>>
  component "OpenPGP on the card\ntouch policy off by default,\n""cached"" = 15s of silence" as PGP <<error>>
  database "attestation ledger\nhost-only: ""<state>/proveo""" as LEDGER <<db>>
  database "sbx secret store\nsealed · no metadata field" as STORE <<db>>
}
component "the agent, in the sandbox\nno path to the authenticator" as AGENT <<app>>
component "**the forgery path**\nforwarded agent · HID passthrough" as HOLE <<error>>

HUMAN ARROW_MAIN AUTH : touch, then PIN
AUTH ARROW_MAIN ALLOW : the signature asserts\nuser verification
ALLOW ARROW_MAIN LEDGER : ""-Y verify"" REFUSES one\nthat does not
LEDGER ARROW_OPTIONAL STORE : beside it, never inside
SOFT ARROW_ERROR ALLOW : signs identically for ANY\nprocess as that user
PGP ARROW_ERROR ALLOW : presence possible, never\nvisible to a verifier
AGENT ARROW_QUEUE LEDGER : may attest ITSELF; a signature\nover its own evidence proves\norigin, not truth
AGENT ARROW_ERROR HOLE
HOLE ARROW_ERROR AUTH : the invariant F2 must test

note bottom of SOFT
  **Custody is not presence.** Any process running
  as that user can sign with it, and on this host
  that includes proveo. A software key proves
  possession and never that a human was there.
end note

note bottom of AUTH
  **Two different assertions.** Presence says
  somebody touched it. Verification adds a PIN or
  biometric and the authenticator reports whether
  it succeeded. Only the second narrows it toward
  WHICH human.
end note

note bottom of ALLOW
  **The class is read here, not claimed.**
  ""verify-required"" needs an sk key, so it
  can only appear on a human line — and the
  VERIFIER requires it. There is no field for a
  signer to lie in.
end note

note bottom of PGP
  **The same hardware, a weaker claim.** A
  card-resident GPG key CAN require touch — but
  the policy ships off, ""cached"" buys 15 silent
  seconds, and none of it travels in the
  signature for a verifier to insist on.
end note

note bottom of HOLE
  **This is the whole experiment.** Forward an
  ssh-agent, pass through a HID device or mount
  ""~/.gnupg"", and an attestation quietly becomes
  a claim again. F2 tests it; nothing else here
  is meaningful without that test passing.
end note

note right of AGENT
  **Deferred, and for a reason.** It can attest
  itself, and that authenticates origin rather
  than truth — so an agent signing its own
  evidence buys attribution, never honesty.
end note

legend bottom
  **What this file settles, and what it only frames**
  ==
  **Settled**: the unit is not a key per operator, it is A SIGNATURE WHOSE PRESENCE
  CLASS THE VERIFIER CAN REQUIRE. A software key proves only custody; a card-resident
  GPG key CAN require touch, but the policy ships off, ""cached"" grants 15 silent
  seconds, ""gpg keytocard"" has been measured resetting it, and none of it appears in
  the signature — so the checker cannot insist. ssh-sk can: ""verify-required"" is an
  allowed_signers OPTION, so ""ssh-keygen -Y verify"" refuses an unverified signature.
  --
  **Settled**: humans and agents need SEPARATE credential profiles, and not by taste.
  The SPIFFE leaf SVID spec and Fulcio's EKU requirements are mutually exclusive
  (spiffe/spiffe#379), so a single profile for both does not exist to be chosen.
  ==
  **Framed, not measured**: five experiments, F1 to F5. F2 is first and cheapest — the
  authenticator must be UNREACHABLE from inside a sandbox, and a pass there is a defect.
  F1 carries the hidden risk: if a ""no-touch-required"" key is indistinguishable to a
  verifier, the guarantee degrades silently and the scheme is decoration.
  --
  **SCOPED**: the experiment is ssh-sk for the HUMAN operator, verified by proveo,
  attesting what set an ""sbx secret"" — one key, one trust store, one namespace, no CA.
  Agents own the TUI transcript and their git commits, and provenance for both is
  DEFERRED, as is the cascade into forks. The rest of this file is background.
  --
  **The step that must not be skipped** is the negative one: sign with a PLAIN key under
  the same principal and confirm ""-Y verify"" REFUSES it. A pass on the touch alone
  measures the hardware; only the refusal measures the guarantee.
  ==
  **All three levels, one format, three key classes** (deferred): a human signs with an sk key
  listed directly and carrying ""verify-required""; an agent and a subagent sign with
  short-lived CERTIFICATES from a CA pinned to ""agent:*"" / ""subagent:*"", in a namespace
  that excludes ""git"" — so they attest provenance and cannot forge a commit.
  --
  **Deferring the agent half is a finding, not caution.** A cert cannot protect an
  agent's own evidence: a signature authenticates ORIGIN,
  not truth: the author is the signer, so a doctored log signs as cleanly as an honest
  one. What already resists tampering does so for a different reason — sandboxd writes
  the network log and the kernel the memory evidence, and neither path is in the mount.
  The transcript is the exception, and the fix there is the MOUNT, not the key.
  --
  **An agent CAN sign, but not where a forge is the verifier.** SSH certificates work in
  the CLI and are unsupported for commit signing by GitHub and GitLab, so per-run identity
  and a Verified badge are mutually exclusive today. And a badge is BINARY: putting agents
  in the ""git"" namespace would not add agent provenance, it would subtract meaning from
  every human signature for third parties who cannot see the principal.
  --
  **First use**: attest what set each ""sbx secret"" — service, fingerprint, when, and a
  PRESENCE CLASS. It answers the sentence the incident was missing: ""ls"" said ""xai""
  existed and was right; nothing said a test fixture had put it there with no human
  anywhere near it.
end legend
@enduml
